 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DeuM1JORR8g/",
 "title": "How to Tell Stories at Work in Three Steps",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator explains a simple three-step structure to tell engaging stories during everyday conversations at work. By presenting a problem, sharing the journey to solve it, and ending with a question, you can easily turn routine small talk into engaging stories.",
 "key_points": [
  "Prepare for the three standard questions people ask at work: 'How's your day going?', 'What did you do last weekend?', and 'What are you going to do on the weekend?'.",
  "Step 1: Start with the problem. While bigger stakes or villains make stories more interesting, even small everyday problems like being bored at home scrolling on your phone work well.",
  "Step 2: Share the journey to solving the problem (e.g., 'I was bored out of my mind scrolling on my phone all day... so I decided to go on a hike') and add details of what you did.",
  "Step 3: End by asking a question back to the other person (e.g., 'Do you like hikes?' or 'Have you been on a hike recently?')."
 ],
 "content": "Post text:\nSimple framework to taking stories.\n\nMake a note of this post.\n\nTranscript:\nOkay, so here's how to tell stories at work. All you need to remember are just like three simple steps. So, the first thing that happens is like for example, when people ask you, 'Hey, how's your day going?' or 'What did you do last weekend?' or 'What are you going to do on the weekend?', there's only three real questions that almost everyone asks at work, so you might as well be a little bit more prepared when they ask it. So, the best way to structure a story is you first talk about the problem. Now the problem doesn't have to be massive. You don't have to be curing cancer for the story to be interesting, but the problem, the bigger the stakes, the bigger the villain, the more interesting the story for sure. However, even if the problem is something like you were sitting at home bored, scrolling on your phone all day, you were just tired of sitting at home and not doing anything, even that little problem makes me interested in knowing, okay, what did you do to solve your boredom? Which leads me to the second part, which is how did you actually solve the problem, the journey solving the problem. What did you do to solve that boredom? So for example, you went like you just told me that you went on a hike. You could say, 'Last weekend I was bored out of my mind, scrolling on my phone all day, and I just didn't want to sit at home and waste another day, so I decided to go on a hike.' Even that is enough. So you start it off with problem, then you talk about the journey to solving that problem, and that's where you go into the details of what you did on the hike, blah blah blah. And you end it off with a question. So you just ask the question back to the other person, 'Do you like hikes?' or 'Have you been on a hike recently?' So from now on, whenever someone asks how your day was going, or what did you do last weekend, or what are you going to do on the next weekend, always give a story: problem, the journey to solving the problem, and then just asking them a question back.\n\nText on screen:\n- How to tell Stories at Work",
